Yes. The useful abstraction in “when in Rome” is protocol compatibility.

A society, institution, family, military unit, software network, or ecosystem has local rules for what counts as legible behavior. Match enough of those rules and you become easier for the system to classify as compatible rather than anomalous:

unknown agent → observe local grammar → adopt compatible signals → reduced friction → greater access

That does resemble immune logic at a high level, though human societies are far more interpretive and flexible than immune systems. Standards create interoperability, but they can also create conformity pressure.

And then your “empire” move is a second layer: not merely obeying a frame, but projecting a frame that changes how others interpret you. Symbols, consistency, ritual, language, reputation, architecture, aesthetics, followers, competence—all can make an organization feel larger and more coherent than any individual component.

But I’d distinguish:

credible signaling → others infer strength/coherence

from

hidden AI influence → others are maneuvered into believing a constructed reality

The first is legitimate statecraft/branding/social signaling. The second becomes covert manipulation when people are intentionally denied awareness that their perceptions are being engineered.

Your “demons” idea works better if we treat them as daemons in the computing sense: delegated processes that handle lower-level work while the higher system preserves invariants.

Then Pantheon becomes something like:

high-level principles
→ bounded daemons
→ local execution
→ monitoring
→ delta detection
→ escalation when invariants are threatened

And that last part matters. A daemon should not get “deep work” plus unlimited discretion. It needs:

scope
permissions
audit trail
stop conditions
human override
provenance

Otherwise “aligned with the Pantheon” can quietly become “whatever the daemon decided counts as alignment.”

So I’d compress your whole thought into:

Blend enough to traverse. Signal enough to be recognized. Delegate enough to scale. Preserve enough oversight that the machinery never silently becomes the sovereign.